"""Version 2: the author's reasoning is explicit; first person is used selectively."""
EDITS = {
0: r"""Changing an embedding model changes the representation of scientific papers. The question is which conclusions change with it. I compare ten models on a fixed corpus of 500,000 OpenAlex records from 26 fields and 2000--2024, using matched panels to examine field centres, internal structure and nearest neighbours, alongside input and representation choices. The models share broad structure but retain only 30.3\% of the 25 nearest neighbours on average within field-period groups. Removing abstracts changes neighbours about as much as changing models; removing titles changes less. A later exploratory analysis of all 325 field pairs finds persistent opposite orderings across models in 262 pairs for angular spread and 221 for effective linear dimension. Requiring relative differences above 5\% leaves 96 and 177. Alternative measures retain many oppositions, with further dependence on centring and pooling. A field comparison can therefore survive repeated article selection while reversing between models. These findings describe geometry, without establishing thematic correctness. I argue that robustness should be assessed at the level of the claim made from a science map: overall agreement can coexist with disagreement on the comparison that matters.""",
1: r"""The same collection of papers can support different accounts of how a research area is organised. With text embeddings, part of that account comes from the model: it turns each paper into a vector, and relationships between those vectors determine which papers appear close. This is useful for locating related work and comparing fields, as applications to biomedical literature and physics show \citep{refbe130e2973,refc0e2cc29e0}. I examine what happens when the papers stay the same and that model changes. The question concerns the conclusions drawn from the representation, beyond whether the resulting maps broadly resemble one another.""",
2: r"""The concern itself is well established. \citet{refa0d2d707e6} asked how much an inferred thematic structure belongs to scientific knowledge and how much to the method that reconstructs it. In \citet{ref6a2f5912bc}, text, citation and hybrid relations produced substantially different cluster memberships even when aggregate accuracy was similar. That distinction motivates the present comparison. Two maps may agree in a summary score and still lead a reader towards different papers or different comparisons between fields.""",
3: r"""I keep a corpus of 500,000 publications fixed: records labelled English in OpenAlex, published in 2000--2024 and assigned to 26 broad Fields. Ten embedding models represent the same papers. The comparisons follow the map from field centres to relationships within fields and then to each article's neighbours. Separate matched subsets vary the input text and the way a model's output becomes one document vector. A subsequent geometric extension asks whether changing the encoder reverses a comparison between two fields, even when its direction persists across article selections.""",
4: r"""The results make that last distinction consequential. Broad relationships are shared, but individual neighbours and geometric field orderings can change. For some comparisons, repeated article selection supports one direction under one model and the opposite direction under another. I retain both the magnitude of those differences and their dependence on processing, because a persistent direction alone says little about the size of the disagreement. The analysis identifies which relationships survive the changes tested. It leaves their thematic validity for a separate assessment.""",
5: r"""Previous comparisons help specify what such an assessment would require. \citet{refc0e2cc29e0} evaluated text and graph representations against a hierarchical classification of physics papers, including title and abstract representations. \citet{refbe130e2973} compared models and output-reduction choices for a large biomedical literature map, using shared field labels among nearby papers as a criterion. In each case, a representation is judged through a particular use and reference. Here I ask whether a conclusion survives a change of representation. Those external evaluations clarify what further evidence would be needed to judge its thematic correctness.""",
6: r"""A model name is only the beginning of a representation recipe. The title and abstract contribute different information, input limits determine how much reaches the model, and token outputs may need to be combined into a document vector. Pooling names that last operation: averaging the outputs and selecting a designated position are different choices. \citet{singh-singh-2022-inefficiency} demonstrated sensitivity to short queries and wording changes in scientific language models. For the present purpose, leaving these choices unspecified would leave part of the mapping procedure outside the comparison.""",
7: r"""Comparing geometry and neighbours together also has direct precedents. \citet{ref4e1e0d5582} did so across retrieval datasets; \citet{ref6da178065c} related local density and asymmetry in different document embeddings and disciplines to citation rates. I build on these approaches by following broad, local and geometric conclusions across a fixed panel. The same article identities, explicit text and representation conditions, and controlled candidate pools make the comparison refer to stated changes rather than an unspecified difference between systems.""",
8: r"""I use agreement and stability for separate questions. Agreement asks what two representations share on the same articles. Stability asks whether a result persists when a specified part of the comparison changes. A field ordering can pass the latter check within every model and still disagree across models. Calling either result thematically valid requires evidence about how the relationships correspond to the research. \citet{QSSreviewQ17} make this interpretive issue clear in invasion biology, where mapping methods can combine different relationships between papers. Neither a repeated result nor a consensus supplies that missing interpretation.""",
9: r"""The object called \emph{shape} must also be specified. All comparisons here precede projection into two dimensions. Field centres describe broad organisation; the articles within each field describe its internal relationships; neighbours identify which papers remain closest to an article. Angular spread and effective linear dimension add descriptions of vector separation and the distribution of variation across directions. I keep these geometric meanings throughout. Greater spread alone cannot establish more diverse topics, and a dimension value cannot be read as a topic count. Each property answers part of the question about the map.""",
11: r"""The scope is descriptive and limited to the selected articles and models. The third question was added in an exploratory extension after the first comparisons; the methods and supplement retain that chronology. OpenAlex labels organise the work, including their known errors, without providing an independent reference for thematic correctness. I therefore assess the persistence of conclusions within this design. The study has no external criterion with which to name the model that recovers a single correct science map.""",
14: r"""I use separate panels for changes that need different controls (Table~\ref{tab:T01}). The three-input panel contains 52,000 articles balanced across Field-period groups, including the 26,000-article pilot. Another panel of 52,000 compares native inputs with the same literal fragment that fits all tokenizers. Equal-size group comparisons address group size, while matched neighbour searches control the search opportunity. Article identities are matched between models throughout. Supplement S1 records extraction, cleaning, counts and the quality flags left in the corpus.""",
15: r"""The ten models provide contrasting scholarly representations, spanning scientific documents, general sentences and word-level biomedical/scientific models (Table~\ref{tab:T02}). I use them as a fixed comparison panel chosen for relevance and training contrasts. They are neither a random sample of encoders nor a ranking of the newest systems. Checkpoints, tokenizer revisions and text formats were fixed before inference. Every model received the same title and abstract through its customary format and native input limit. MiniLM's native 256-token limit remains primary, with a separate 512-token sensitivity. Native truncation affected 49.58\% of MiniLM inputs and 20.16\% of MPNet inputs.""",
18: r"""A centre is the mean of a group's unit-length article vectors. This deliberately reduces a collection of articles to one object for the broad comparison. Within-group comparisons retain the article-level variation. To check how much agreement can accompany averaging itself, random groups preserve the observed sizes and publication periods but discard the observed allocation of labels. The random allocation is shared across models.""",
22: r"""For Field geometry, I compare every unordered pair of the 26 Fields, giving 325 pairs per property. A direction is persistent for a model only when it holds for the primary 2,000 articles per Field, twenty half samples of 1,000 and five further selections of 2,000 from the same corpus. Agreement of all ten requires that common persistent direction in every model. Opposition requires at least two fixed models that persistently support opposite directions. Everything else is unresolved, a category that does not imply equality. Relative magnitude is $2(B-A)/(A+B)$, with $A$ and $B$ the two Field values. Minima of 0, 1, 5 and 10\% show how the classification depends on small differences.""",
23: r"""These repetitions test what happens when articles change within the corpus already collected. Their overlapping selections do not produce independent population samples or confidence intervals. The operational screens preserve excessive median shifts and broad selection ranges as alerts, with separate rules and denominators in the supplement. The pair comparisons also share their components: a model belongs to several model pairs, and a Field to several Field pairs. I report their counts descriptively and use no significance test that treats the pairs as independent.""",
24: r"""The comparisons were developed in stages, and the order matters for their interpretation. The initial shape and neighbour analysis was followed by the expanded input panel, matched Subfield searches and case atlas. Morphology came later as an exploratory extension; the all-pairs summary followed its initial examples. Each new calculation had rules recorded locally beforehand, but the whole study was not externally preregistered. Some controls, including random-group centres and a fixed-neighbour-fraction comparison, followed earlier results. Supplement S2 keeps that sequence explicit. Supplements S3--S7 contain the time, composition, model-trait, source-error and processing checks, including those that constrain the conclusions.""",
26: r"""The internal comparison gives a flatter result. Mean CKA stays close to 0.63 in Fields and 0.64 in the same 183 Subfields eligible at every size (Figure~\ref{fig:scales}B). Moving from 128 to 512 articles scarcely changes those means. A more specific label consequently does not imply less agreement here. The large difference from the centre panel needs a narrower reading: group centres and individual articles are different objects, so the panels cannot estimate a causal effect of scale.""",
30: r"""It would also be misleading to treat shape and neighbours as unrelated tests. Across the 45 model-pair means in the initial analysis, their descriptive rank correlation is 0.920. Their joint use has a different justification. CKA describes relationships distributed through the space, whereas neighbour overlap tells us whether a reader would encounter the same articles. Both matter for interpretation even when they move together.""",
32: r"""An input decision can be as consequential as the choice of encoder. For the same 52,000 articles, changing models gives a mean shape change of 0.370, measured as one minus corrected CKA. Keeping the model but leaving only the title gives 0.366. The corresponding neighbour-replacement fractions are 68.8\% and 70.7\% (Figure~\ref{fig:input}). I read these close averages as evidence that the two choices can have comparable consequences within this design. They do not establish equivalence between their effects.""",
34: r"""That comparison itself depends on pooling. Under the principal mean rule, removing the title has a relatively modest effect, while removing the abstract slightly exceeds model change in mean neighbour replacement. With CLS or SEP for the four word-level BERTs, the latter ordering reverses. These scenarios move four representations at once, including the cross-model reference. They cannot isolate a pure pooling effect. Selecting the readout that makes the models agree most would change the purpose of the comparison.""",
36: r"""The geometry extension asks what happens to a specific statement about two Fields when its representation changes. Before comparing models, article selection provides an internal check. Angular spread retains no alerts across the 260 model--Field cases in either the half-sample or equal-size screen. Effective linear dimension retains four half-sample alerts, though none occurs in the five equal-size selections (Table S12). I keep those four cases visible; the second check does not cancel the first.""",
42: r"""Connection did not meet the interpretive requirement. Synthetic elongated clouds and isolated extreme points could produce signals resembling fragmentation, while sample size and neighbour count substantially altered the measurements. The equal-size screen retained 51 connection alerts among 260 cases. I therefore report the connection results and their failed construct checks in Supplement S6, without using them to infer that a Field contains more separate thematic groups.""",
43: r"""The most useful result is the coexistence of shared structure and persistent disagreement on a particular claim. Centre agreement exceeds the random-group reference. Yet models that share that broad organisation can repeatedly order a pair of Fields in opposite ways as the articles change. This is why I would attach a robustness assessment to the comparison being interpreted. A stable neighbour set, a relationship between groups and an ordering on a geometric property each require their own check; an overall score cannot settle all of them.""",
44: r"""That reading builds on an established concern in science mapping \citep{refa0d2d707e6,ref6a2f5912bc}. \citet{refc0e2cc29e0} evaluate representations against a specified external classification. \citet{ref4e1e0d5582} compare the shared geometry and retrieval relationships of representations. Here I connect these levels to the persistence and size of concrete Field comparisons under matched articles. The contribution rests on that connection and its controls. The measures of agreement and the concern with representation choice both have clear precedents.""",
45: r"""The geometric measures are helpful within that deliberately limited interpretation. An angle describes separation; PR describes how variance occupies linear directions. Calling either interdisciplinarity or semantic diversity would add a claim about content that these calculations have not tested. Work on embedding isotropy raises a related problem of matching a geometric quantity to its interpretation \citep{rudman-etal-2022-isoscore}. The connection checks show the practical cost: repeatable differences between clouds were insufficient grounds for the proposed fragmentation reading.""",
46: r"""For a science-mapping application, I would begin with the comparison that the reader is expected to interpret. A retrieval claim needs a specified candidate pool and neighbour count. A geometric Field comparison needs an explicit representation rule, article balance and minimum difference. These choices should guide the robustness check. The input experiment also gives a concrete reporting lesson: the model name should be accompanied by the title/abstract choice, text limit and pooling rule, because each helps determine the representation being interpreted.""",
47: r"""The supplementary analyses resist an easy account of why models disagree. Medicine has lower shape agreement than Energy, yet is not consistently lowest for neighbours. The Medicine--Energy difference remains after balancing Subfields and after removing BioBERT and PubMedBERT (Supplement S5). Those checks narrow possible explanations without establishing a unique cause. Model families are also readout-dependent. Since architecture, training source and objective vary together across these encoders, the observed similarities cannot assign a separate causal role to any one of them.""",
48: r"""Time makes the dependence on comparison design especially clear. Mean shape agreement increases slightly between the earliest and latest periods. Mean neighbour agreement falls by 0.87 percentage points with all candidates and rises by 0.91 points with 2,048 candidates in each group. The search opportunity changes the sign. I would therefore avoid reading this as evidence that science has historically converged. The models use current parameters to represent old and new publications; the calculation does not reconstruct the knowledge available when those papers appeared.""",
49: r"""The claims stop at the corpus as well as the comparison. These are selected OpenAlex records with usable abstracts and predominantly English text. Reinforcement, balanced panels and equal group weights support comparisons between Fields, without estimating the composition of world research. The atlas identifies classification and content errors that survive cleaning. The checks address named failure modes rather than certify every record. Saved selections and checkpoints make the work inspectable, but repeating selections cannot correct what the corpus fails to cover.""",
50: r"""The ten encoders provide a bounded set of alternatives. Several share architectures, training sources and objectives, and several are not specialised similarity models. Restricting the analysis to six similarity-oriented encoders or changing readouts addresses some concerns without producing a representative sample of models. Persistent opposition establishes a dependence within this panel. Deciding which direction better describes the underlying research would require evidence beyond that opposition.""",
51: r"""The sequence of the work is another limit I retain. The geometry extension and all-pairs summary followed earlier findings; complete records do not change their exploratory status. For the next step, I would choose one consequential comparison and assess it against independently judged article relationships or a discipline-specific question. This would address the connection between geometry and subject matter that remains open. Adding encoders alone would extend the agreement comparison without supplying that connection.""",
52: r"""Broad agreement between models can coexist with different neighbours and opposite geometric orderings of Fields. Input choice can be as consequential as changing the model, and repeated article selection can preserve a result that reverses between encoders. I therefore favour testing the claim drawn from the map alongside overall similarity. Angular spread and effective linear dimension make that possible for specific geometric comparisons, provided their definitions, magnitudes and processing sensitivities stay explicit. The remaining step is substantive: interpreting those model-produced geometries as properties of scientific knowledge requires further validation.""",
}
